"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 0.2 and the surgical finding of positive margins. The pathological stage was determined as pT2c with Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1). A significant limitation is that no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status cannot be assessed, which widens uncertainty. Furthermore, there is no documented biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison available.\n\nThe prognosis would be significantly revised by obtaining postoperative PSA levels and tracking their subsequent trajectory over time. The absence of prior follow-up data limits the current assessment. Given the positive margins despite low preoperative PSA and favorable initial pathology findings, ongoing surveillance guided by future PSA measurements will be crucial for risk stratification. The fixed estimated horizon is 76.69 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 46.02–107.37 months; its coverage has not been validated."
